import{_ as s,o as a,c as t,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.BvXRlzVA.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Compounds and their identifiers","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"30-manual/35-compounds.md","filePath":"30-manual/35-compounds.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"30-manual/35-compounds.md"};function o(h,e,d,l,r,p){return a(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="compounds" tabindex="-1">Compounds and their identifiers <a class="header-anchor" href="#compounds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Compounds and their identifiers {#compounds}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The ThermoML Archive names its compounds as each article did, and a few of them have no InChIKey at all. The compound database is a second, separate table that knows every way of naming a compound: all its names, its CAS number, its PubChem CID, its formula, its molar mass, its SMILES, its InChI and its InChIKey. It lets you find a compound from any of them, links the compounds of the archive to it, and is the place to complete what an article left out.</p><p>Nothing here changes the ThermoML data. The archive stays exactly as NIST publishes it, and when the two disagree both are shown and you choose.</p><h2 id="Two-databases" tabindex="-1">Two databases <a class="header-anchor" href="#Two-databases" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two databases {#Two-databases}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The package keeps two databases, side by side and never mixed:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Database</th><th style="text-align:left;">What it holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">Where it comes from</th><th style="text-align:left;">Can you change it?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">the <strong>thermophysical database</strong> (<code>database()</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">the experimental points: properties, conditions, uncertainties, samples, references</td><td style="text-align:left;">the ThermoML Archive of NIST/TRC</td><td style="text-align:left;">no, it is the reference; you only add your own points as another source</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">the <strong>compound database</strong> (<code>compound_database()</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">the nomenclature and the base properties of the compounds: every name, CAS number, PubChem CID, formula, molar mass, SMILES, InChI and InChIKey</td><td style="text-align:left;">lists derived from PubChem, fixed at one commit</td><td style="text-align:left;">the lists are never touched; the compounds you add or complete with <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.register_compound!"><code>register_compound!</code></a> go to a separate file of your own (<code>custom.jld2</code>) and are loaded together with the lists, as one database</td></tr></tbody></table><p>What you add is kept apart on your computer and never written into the lists or into the archive: <code>register_compound!</code> changes the database in the session, and <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.save_compounds"><code>save_compounds</code></a> writes only the compounds you added or changed to <code>custom.jld2</code>. Every call of <code>compound_database()</code> reads the lists and then lays that file over them, so the variable you work with is a single database. <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.link_compounds"><code>link_compounds</code></a> joins the two in a <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.LinkedDatabase"><code>LinkedDatabase</code></a>. The join only records which compound of the compound database describes each compound of the archive; it never renames, merges or corrects anything in the archive. Where the two disagree you see both and you choose.</p><h2 id="Getting-it" tabindex="-1">Getting it <a class="header-anchor" href="#Getting-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Getting it {#Getting-it}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">cdb </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> compound_database</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The first call downloads six lists of identifiers (about 47 MB) from a fixed commit of the <a href="https://github.com/CalebBell/chemicals" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">chemicals</a> repository, checks each file against its SHA-256 digest, parses them and saves a binary cache (about 52 MB). Later calls read the cache and never use the network. The lists hold about 76 000 compounds: the largest part comes from PubChem, according to the authors of the repository, and the others add salts and hydrates, the ions of brines (<code>Na+</code>, <code>Cl-</code>, <code>Ca+2</code>) and compounds that the PubChem list lacks (<code>cesium formate</code>). See the <a href="https://github.com/Eduardo-BDMAlves/ThermophysicalDB.jl/blob/main/NOTICE.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">notices</a> for the origin and the terms.</p><p>The files live in <code>compounds/</code> inside the <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/30-manual/40-data-management#updates">data directory</a>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">File</th><th style="text-align:left;">Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>seed/*.tsv</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the downloaded lists, verified against their digests</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>seed.jld2</code>, <code>seed.toml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the parsed lists, and a description written last (the commit and the digests they were built from)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>pubchem/CID-*.json</code>, <code>pubchem_missing.toml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">what PubChem answered when you asked it (one file for each compound), and the questions it could not answer, see <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/30-manual/35-compounds#compounds_pubchem">Asking PubChem</a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>custom.jld2</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the compounds that you added or changed</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If the download fails, the error says where to get the files in a browser; copy them with the same names into <code>compounds/seed/</code> and call <code>compound_database()</code> again. Files with the right digest are used as they are. The commit is fixed, so everybody who installs the same version of the package gets the same lists. <code>rebuild = true</code> ignores the cache and parses the lists again.</p><h2 id="Looking-a-compound-up" tabindex="-1">Looking a compound up <a class="header-anchor" href="#Looking-a-compound-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Looking a compound up {#Looking-a-compound-up}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.lookup_compounds"><code>lookup_compounds</code></a> takes a name or any identifier:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;7732-18-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)              </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># CAS number</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;XLYOFNOQVPJJNP-UHFFFAOYSA-N&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)  </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># InChIKey (case does not matter)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;962&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)                    </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># PubChem CID</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)      </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># InChI</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;cesium formate&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)         </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a name, any spelling</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;H2O&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)                    </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a formula, as the lists write it</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;CO&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; by </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :smiles</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)       </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a SMILES must be declared</span></span></code></pre></div><p>With the default <code>by = :auto</code> the kind is recognised from how the text is written: an InChIKey, a CAS number, a text that starts with <code>InChI=</code> or <code>SMILES=</code>. Anything else is tried as a name, then as a CID (only digits), then as a formula. A SMILES cannot be told from a name by looking at it (<code>&quot;CO&quot;</code> is carbon monoxide as a formula and methanol as a SMILES), so it needs <code>by = :smiles</code> or the prefix <code>SMILES=</code>. The <code>match</code> column of the result says which kind matched.</p><p>Names follow the rules of <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.find_compounds"><code>find_compounds</code></a>: case, accents, hyphens and spaces are ignored, <code>&quot;n-hexane&quot;</code> also finds <code>&quot;hexane&quot;</code> and the usual alternative spellings are understood. When a name finds nothing, close names are listed (<code>:contains</code>, <code>:similar</code>); an identifier that finds nothing returns nothing, never a guess.</p><p>SMILES and formulas are compared <strong>as written</strong>. A valid SMILES written in another way than the lists use it, or a formula with the elements in another order, does not match; no structure is parsed. <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.compound_record"><code>compound_record</code></a> returns everything known about one compound, including all its names.</p><p>The first search by name in a session builds an index of the names (about three seconds and 250 MB of memory for the whole database, measured on the 2026 lists); the searches by identifier and the later ones are instant.</p><h2 id="Linking-it-to-the-archive" tabindex="-1">Linking it to the archive <a class="header-anchor" href="#Linking-it-to-the-archive" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Linking it to the archive {#Linking-it-to-the-archive}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.link_compounds"><code>link_compounds</code></a> matches every compound of the archive with the database:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">db </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> database</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">linked </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> link_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(db, cdb)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">linked</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">links          </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># the table of links</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The InChIKey is tried first, then the InChI, then the names. <code>linked.links</code> has one row for each pair (compound of the archive, compound of the database that may describe it), and says how reliable the pair is:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Column</th><th style="text-align:left;">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>via</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>:inchikey</code> or <code>:inchi</code> (the same species), <code>:name</code> (a name matched) or <code>:none</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>candidates</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">how many rows the compound has; more than one means you have to choose</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>formula_check</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the formulas compared by their elements: <code>:same</code>, <code>:different</code>, <code>:unknown</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>inchi_check</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the InChIs compared: <code>:same</code>, <code>:stereo</code> (the same skeleton with other stereochemistry or isotopes), <code>:different</code>, <code>:unknown</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A link through the InChIKey or the InChI is the same species. <strong>A link through a name is only a lead</strong>: names are shared between species. In the archive of 2020-09-30, <code>&quot;sulfur&quot;</code> leads to hydrogen sulfide, <code>&quot;(E)-3-hexene&quot;</code> to <code>cis-3-hexene</code> and a hydrate to its anhydrous salt. Trust a <code>:name</code> link only when <code>formula_check</code> and <code>inchi_check</code> are <code>:same</code>; <code>:stereo</code> and <code>:different</code> mean a related compound, not the same one. <code>link_compounds(db, cdb; by_name = false)</code> links by InChIKey and InChI only. The archive is never changed, and nothing is disambiguated in it.</p><p>On that archive (8 526 compounds, 8 517 of them with an InChIKey), 4 363 were found by InChIKey, 293 only by name and 3 870 were not found, and the compounds found account for 87 % of the appearances of a component in a measurement. The compounds that are missing are, above all, ionic liquids and uncommon organic compounds that the lists do not cover, and some of them, such as 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate, are among the most measured. The measurement was made on that date and changes with the archive and the lists.</p><p><a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.find_compounds"><code>find_compounds</code></a> understands the database too, when you give it as the second argument, or the linked database as the first:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">find_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(db, cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;7732-18-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># water in the archive, from its CAS number</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">find_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(linked, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;oxidane&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)      </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># the same, from a synonym that no article used</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The ThermoML compounds with the same InChIKey as any compound the text identifies are found first. A compound of the archive that has no InChIKey is reached only through its own names, so nothing is guessed.</p><h2 id="compounds_linked_search" tabindex="-1">Searching the archive with any name <a class="header-anchor" href="#compounds_linked_search" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Searching the archive with any name {#compounds_linked_search}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.LinkedDatabase"><code>LinkedDatabase</code></a> takes the place of the archive in <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.points"><code>points</code></a> and <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.mixture"><code>mixture</code></a>, and a compound can be named by anything either database knows:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">points</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(linked; component </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;oxidane&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, property </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Mass density&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a synonym</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">points</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(linked; component </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;7732-18-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, temperature </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">280</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">300</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">))  </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a CAS number</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">mixture</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(linked, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;7732-18-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;64-17-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)                            </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># water + ethanol</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The result is an ordinary <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.DataView"><code>DataView</code></a> of the archive, so everything else works on it as before. Because a view does not remember the compound database, refine it with the names that the archive itself uses.</p><h3 id="When-a-name-is-ambiguous" tabindex="-1">When a name is ambiguous <a class="header-anchor" href="#When-a-name-is-ambiguous" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When a name is ambiguous {#When-a-name-is-ambiguous}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A text that leads to one compound of the archive is used as it is. A text that leads to several is not guessed: a formula such as <code>&quot;C4H10&quot;</code> belongs to two isomers, and a name can be used for different species. The candidates are listed, with their formula, InChIKey, CAS number, other names and number of points, ordered by number of points and then by name, so the same number means the same compound every time on the same data:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>&quot;C4H10&quot; matches 2 compounds of the dataset:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  1. n-butane  |  C4H10  |  IJDNQMDRQITEOD-UHFFFAOYSA-N  |  CAS 106-97-8  |  5 points</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  2. isobutane  |  C4H10  |  NNPPMTNAJDCUHE-UHFFFAOYSA-N  |  CAS 75-28-5  |  1 points</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Which one? (1-2, a for all of them, q to cancel):</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Answer with the number, <code>a</code> for all of them (they then count as the same component, as in <code>points(db; component = &quot;C4H10&quot;)</code>), or <code>q</code> to cancel. An invalid answer is asked again. A text with no exact candidate but close ones (a misspelling) is listed in the same way, without the <code>a</code> option: a close name is never taken without your answer.</p><p>The keyword <code>choose</code> of <code>points</code> and <code>mixture</code> controls this:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;"><code>choose</code></th><th style="text-align:left;">Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>:ask</code> (default)</td><td style="text-align:left;">asks in the terminal when the session is interactive (<code>isinteractive()</code> is <code>true</code>, as in the REPL and in VS Code); otherwise throws an <code>ArgumentError</code> that lists the candidates, because a script that nobody watches must not choose</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>:error</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">never asks; throws the same error</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>:all</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">takes every exact candidate; a text with only close candidates is still refused</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>Dict(&quot;butane&quot; =&gt; 2, &quot;C4H10&quot; =&gt; :all)</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">an answer prepared for each text, by the number the menu shows, so a script does not stop; a text that is not in the dictionary is handled as with <code>:ask</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The question is read from <code>input</code> and written to <code>output</code> (<code>stdin</code> and <code>stdout</code> by default), which is how it is tested. The answers you give are kept in <code>linked.choices</code> and used again for the same text during the session; <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.forget_choices!"><code>forget_choices!</code></a> clears them. Nothing is written to disk, and the archive is never changed.</p><h2 id="compounds_pubchem" tabindex="-1">Asking PubChem <a class="header-anchor" href="#compounds_pubchem" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Asking PubChem {#compounds_pubchem}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The lists do not cover everything: ionic liquids and uncommon organic compounds are the usual gaps. <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.fetch_pubchem!"><code>fetch_pubchem!</code></a> asks <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PubChem</a> about a compound and adds the answer to the database:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">fetch_pubchem!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a name</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">fetch_pubchem!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;64-17-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)                                        </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a CAS number</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">fetch_pubchem!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;SMILES=CCO&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)                                     </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a SMILES</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">fetch_pubchem!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;702&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)                                            </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a PubChem CID</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>The network is used only when you ask for it</strong>, with this function or with <code>online = true</code>: the package never reaches PubChem by itself, and a session without <code>online = true</code> works offline. Even then, nothing is asked that the database already knows: if the text matches a compound exactly (a name, CAS number, CID, InChIKey, InChI or SMILES), those rows are returned without a request. <code>force = true</code> asks PubChem anyway, for example to complete a row that has no InChIKey; <code>refresh = true</code> also forces the question and writes the answer again.</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">lookup_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;bmim bf4&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">; online </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)       </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># asks PubChem only if the database does not know it</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">points</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(linked; component </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;64-17-5&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, online </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># the same, when searching the archive</span></span></code></pre></div><p>What PubChem answers is kept as one JSON file for each compound in <code>compounds/pubchem/</code>, with the date and the question. That folder is the separate table of what came from PubChem: every time the database loads, the files are read and laid over the lists, and the compounds you register go on top of both, so the order of precedence is <strong>lists, then PubChem, then your own compounds</strong>. The files are plain JSON, so you can read them, and deleting one (or the whole folder) removes what it brought without touching your own compounds. A compound that PubChem described is marked <code>source = &quot;pubchem&quot;</code>, and the text you asked about is kept as one of its names.</p><p>A name or a CAS number can belong to several compounds (salts, hydrates, stereoisomers). Then they are listed in the order PubChem gives, with the CID, the IUPAC name, the formula, the InChIKey and the molar mass, and you choose with the number, <code>a</code> for all of them or <code>q</code> to cancel, exactly as in the <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/30-manual/35-compounds#compounds_linked_search">search with any name</a>. The same <code>choose</code> keyword works: <code>:ask</code> (the default, which asks only in an interactive session), <code>:error</code>, <code>:all</code>, or a <code>Dict(&quot;glucose&quot; =&gt; 2)</code> of prepared answers; in <code>points(linked; online = true)</code> the prepared answers for the PubChem menu use the key <code>&quot;pubchem:glucose&quot;</code>, because the dataset has a menu of its own.</p><p>A question that PubChem cannot answer is remembered for 180 days in <code>pubchem_missing.toml</code> and not asked again, so a name that does not exist does not cost a request every time; <code>refresh = true</code> asks again, and also asks again for the compounds that are already kept. Problems with the service never turn into a &quot;not found&quot;: if PubChem is busy or unreachable the package retries a few times and then throws a <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.PubChemError"><code>ThermophysicalDB.PubChemError</code></a> that says what happened; nothing is changed or remembered.</p><p>PubChem&#39;s usage policy limits the requests to 5 per second and 400 per minute, and the package waits between requests to stay below that: 3 per second by default, which <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.pubchem_rate!"><code>pubchem_rate!</code></a> changes. Read the <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/docs/programmatic-access" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">usage policy</a> of PubChem before sending many requests. The data of PubChem come from many contributors who may have terms of their own (see <code>NOTICE.md</code>); the package keeps what it fetches only on your computer.</p><h3 id="Filling-the-gaps-in-advance" tabindex="-1">Filling the gaps in advance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Filling-the-gaps-in-advance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Filling the gaps in advance {#Filling-the-gaps-in-advance}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The compounds that matter most to your searches are the ones that the archive measured most, and some of them, ionic liquids in particular, are not in the lists. <a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.prefetch_compounds!"><code>prefetch_compounds!</code></a> asks PubChem about them in one go: it orders the compounds of the archive that have an InChIKey by number of points and looks at the first ones (<code>top = 200</code> by default); only those that the database does not know, and that PubChem has not already failed to answer, are asked. <code>top</code> is therefore a rank among the most measured compounds, not a number of requests: once those compounds are known, nothing is asked.</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">db </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> database</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">cdb </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> compound_database</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">prefetch_compounds!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(db, cdb; top </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 100</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">linked </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> link_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(db, cdb)       </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># again, to include what was fetched</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It asks by InChIKey, so the answer is exactly the compound of the archive and there is nothing to choose; the compounds of the archive that have no InChIKey cannot be asked this way. What it fetches is kept like any other answer (one JSON file for each compound, and the questions PubChem could not answer are remembered for 180 days), so the run can be interrupted and repeated without asking twice. It sends about one request for each compound at 3 per second, so 200 compounds take about a minute. The result says how many compounds were considered, how many of them were unknown, and how many were requested, fetched, not found and skipped because PubChem was known to lack them (<code>considered</code>, <code>unknown</code>, <code>requested</code>, <code>fetched</code>, <code>not_found</code>, <code>remembered_missing</code>).</p><p>To do it as soon as the archive is ready, ask <code>database()</code>:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">db </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> database</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(; prefetch_compounds </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This is off by default, because it needs the network and downloads the compound database if it is not there yet. If PubChem cannot be reached it only warns, and the database is returned as usual. If the 200 most measured compounds are already in the database (or remembered as missing), the call asks nothing.</p><h2 id="Adding-your-own-compounds" tabindex="-1">Adding your own compounds <a class="header-anchor" href="#Adding-your-own-compounds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adding your own compounds {#Adding-your-own-compounds}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/ThermophysicalDB.jl/previews/PR12/95-reference#ThermophysicalDB.register_compound!"><code>register_compound!</code></a> adds a compound that the lists lack, or completes one they have. What you give replaces what the row had, and your first name becomes its main name:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">register_compound!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    names </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;cesium formate&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;caesium formate&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    inchikey </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;WLHQEXWBUBNKIO-UHFFFAOYSA-N&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    cas </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;3495-36-1&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    molar_mass </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 177.92</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,          </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># g/mol, for example from the certificate of the supplier</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">save_compounds</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb)               </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># without this, the compound exists only in this session</span></span></code></pre></div><p>By default <code>register_compound!</code> changes the database only in the session, so that nothing is written to your disk without you asking. Pass <code>save = true</code> to write at once, in the same call:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">register_compound!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(cdb; names </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;cesium formate&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">], molar_mass </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 177.92</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, save </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>With <code>save = true</code> all the compounds that are yours (this one and the ones added before in the session) are written to <code>custom.jld2</code>, in the folder the database was loaded from, or in <code>dir = &quot;...&quot;</code> if you give one. The lists are never written to.</p><p>A compound needs an InChIKey or a name. The rows you added or changed are marked (<code>custom</code>) and kept in <code>custom.jld2</code>; they are laid over the lists again every time the database loads, so a new version of the lists does not remove them. A molar mass that you give is the one the conversions of concentration will use first.</p><h2 id="Limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#Limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits {#Limits}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>The lists come from a third party and are fixed at one commit; they are not updated by the package. A new commit comes with a new version of ThermophysicalDB.jl.</p></li><li><p>Names are ambiguous, identifiers are exact: prefer the InChIKey and check the <code>formula_check</code> and <code>inchi_check</code> of a link made through a name.</p></li><li><p>The molar mass of a row is that of the entry as the lists describe it. A hydrate and its anhydrous salt are different entries; choose the one that your sample is, or give the molar mass yourself.</p></li><li><p>PubChem is asked only on request (<code>fetch_pubchem!</code>, <code>online = true</code>), and only by name, CAS number, CID, InChIKey, InChI or SMILES, not by formula. Its answers are as good as its records: names, CAS numbers and molar masses can be wrong, as in any database, so check the ones that matter.</p></li></ul>`,66)])])}const u=s(n,[["render",o]]);export{k as __pageData,u as default};
